[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is an Umbrel. It\'s a small computer with no screen and no keyboard. It just sits in your home and becomes your cloud. So everything you would normally keep in iCloud, Google Drive or Dropbox lives here instead. And it does a lot more than that.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'umbrelOS is the software that runs on it. You can install it for free on pretty much any spare computer or a NAS that you already have. And today, we are releasing our biggest update to umbrelOS.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So this photo is about to travel about 400 miles to a server farm. right over there'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to your own home cloud. with an Umbrel Home or an Umbrel Pro on almost anything.'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '..that\'s been sitting in your closet. Then, you open your web browser, and it comes to life — finding your storage drives'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and combining them into And now, with Photos and our new mobile app, backs itself up the moment you take it.'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That old box of hard drives? Plug them in, and import decades of memories. Your Umbrel also shows up on your computer like any other drive. ...or drop in entire folders'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from your computer. One click! And it all starts coming home. You can also add accounts'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for everyone in your home, and each person gets a place for their own files, their own photos, or a shared space for all of you together. And this is where it really opens up.. Because there are over'

[01:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '400 apps in the Umbrel App Store. AI agents, local models, media streaming, a Bitcoin node...? You name it.'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Windows, Linux, even Android! Oh, and see that cursor?'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s an AI agent using one. So that\'s umbrelOS 2.0!'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s powerful, it\'s beautiful, and it just makes running your home cloud such a joy. There\'s a lot in it. So let\'s dive in. Let\'s start with Files because that\'s where most people'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start with their home cloud. Files has always been the simple part of Umbrel. You drag something in. And it\'s on your cloud. What\'s new in 2.0 is that it can also pull in'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whatever you\'ve got sitting in Google Drive, Dropbox, iCloud Drive, whatever. So pick an account. We could bring down everything or just pick a specific folder'

[03:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it downloads to your Umbrel. You can do that once or have it keep the folder up to date. So anything new comes over on its own. But the bigger change in 2.0 is Photos, which is brand new. It scans the folders on your Umbrel'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like the one I just dropped in, or whatever\'s coming down from Google Drive and indexes everything right where it is. Nothing gets moved around. And if you plug in an SD card or this drive,'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it can pull those photos off too. And then you get this stunning experience to browse everything in one place. Years, months... ...days, albums. Or just describe the photo you\'re after,'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it finds it. And of course, all that runs locally right here on your Umbrel. Under the hood, it\'s powered by a small specialized AI model. Oh, and with the new Umbrel app for mobile,'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your whole camera roll backs up here automatically. And because all of this lives in Files,'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s nothing proprietary about any of it. They\'re just your photos in folders, in whatever format they come in with no lock-in or anything. Now, this drive also has our whole movie collection on it.'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Getting that into an app like Jellyfin or Plex is going to be quite a chore. Well, in 2.0, you can just ask. Yeah, that\'s the other new thing.'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'umbrelOS now has an MCP server, so you can connect to whatever agents you already use: Claude Code, Codex, OpenClaw, Hermes, and decide exactly what it\'s allowed to touch. That can be small.'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Reading one folder or making itself a folder to keep its own files in. Or it can be everything. All your apps. So it can troubleshoot, install things, whatever you would normally do yourself... ...which opens up some fun ideas.'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'What it\'s using there is'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is also brand new in 2.0. You can run full operating systems on your Umbrel now, like Windows, Ubuntu, Android, and use them right from your browser on any device.'

[06:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Each one runs in isolation, away from your apps and your files, and your AI agents can use them the same way that you would. Browse the web, install apps, store their own files, whatever the task needs.'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s a full computer of their own. And you can watch it work or step in whenever you want. A lot of the work in 2.0 went into making things'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like this feel simple and delightful to use. Suj can tell you more about that. While designing 2.0, we had one question for every single screen: Can you get through it'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without reading up anything? With no manual, no docs, no idea what a NAS or a home server is. Even setting up umbrelOS asks you nothing technical. It finds the storage drives,'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tells you what it recommends, and you say yes, or pick something else. And for anything that takes a while, there\'s no hiding behind a spinner. You\'ll see things happen in real time. Like importing from Dropbox,'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'backing up your Umbrel, uploading your photos, expanding your storage. There\'s always a LiveTile telling you where it\'s at. And then there\'s the rest of it. The stuff you\'ll see and interact with the most...'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in a new, refined design language. The homescreen, the widgets, The home screen, the widgets, the dock, notifications, the App Store, Settings,'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all of it. And because everyone in the house gets their own account on your home cloud, everyone gets their own Umbrel that\'s just unique to them. None of that needs explaining on how to use, and that\'s the point.'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You just start using it and it does what you expected. And it works that way everywhere you use umbrelOS. You see a new design once, then you live with it every day. So we\'ve spent a long time'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the last ‘one percent’. Every icon and window has been redrawn. Things move like... ...they almost have weight to them. The widgets and dock are a little more translucent,'

[08:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'letting your wallpaper come through. Change it, and the whole experience takes on a different mood. On their own, none of these matter much, but together it\'s the difference between'

[08:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something you just use... ...and something you actually enjoy using. Over to Luke. Everything on this table is running the same umbrelOS 2.0 A Raspberry Pi, a NAS, a mini PC,'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an AI PC, and our own Umbrel Home and Umbrel Pro. The same software, the same experience on all of them. So if you\'ve got a computer lying around, there\'s a good chance it can become your Umbrel.'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'With the new Storage Manager, you can take whatever drives you\'ve got SSDs or hard drives, and combine them into one big block of storage or you can enable FailSafe mode, and umbrelOS will spread'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your data across the drives. So if any one of them fails, you don\'t lose anything. Just replace the drive and it carries on. And there\'s a nice trick in here if you\'re running a NAS with hard drives, and it happens to have a slot or two for SSDs.'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can enable This is a lot more than just a simple cache. umbrelOS actually understands your data, works out where each piece should live and moves things between'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the SSDs and hard drives in real time as your usage changes. It\'s so efficient that even a small SSD like 256GB can accelerate something like a 16TB hard drive. On a typical hard drive NAS,'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we see around a 30x speed-up with SSD acceleration. And for some workloads, it\'s even on par with an all-SSD setup. GPU support is also new in 2.0 So if there\'s an NVIDIA, AMD,'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or an Intel graphics card in your device, or an eGPU plugged in over Thunderbolt, umbrelOS finds it, and hands it to any app that can use it with nothing to configure, no drivers, no command line.'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So you can run serious local AI models with Ollama, transcode 4K videos in Jellyfin, and even let Frigate do object detection on your home security cameras. Underneath all of that,'

[10:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s a lot of engineering in 2.0, for example, a new hash-based virtual filesystem, natural-language search across your Umbrel using small local AI models and a lot more. That\'s its own an engineering video,'

[10:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and we\'re looking forward to that one. So yeah, umbrelOS 2.0 is out today. You can install it on your own hardware, or get an Umbrel Home or Umbrel Pro and just plug them in.'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And if you are new to all this, you can start small. Just back up your photos. The rest can come... ...whenever you\'re ready.'
